#!/usr/bin/env bash
# The checks, run by the commit itself.
#
# Running them by hand and then committing is two steps, and the result of the
# first can be lost between them. It has been, twice: once when a clippy run
# reused a build fingerprint and reported clean, and once when the output was
# piped somewhere and the pipeline's exit status was the pipe's rather than the
# check's. Both put a commit on the branch that CI then failed. This closes the
# gap by making the commit itself the thing that runs them.
#
# Turned on by `git config core.hooksPath .githooks`, which `scripts/check.sh`
# offers the first time it is run.
#
# # Why `commit-msg` and not `pre-commit`
#
# This used to be a `pre-commit` hook, and it moved on 2026-08-31 because of
# what that cost. `pre-commit` runs before the commit message exists, so the
# gate could not read it, so it could not tell the RED half of red/green from
# any other commit. It therefore refused every commit whose tests fail, which is
# every RED commit, and red/green is the rule this project holds above all the
# others.
#
# That was not noticed when the hook was turned on. Phase 1 committed failing
# tests freely because there was no hook; turning it on closed a gate nobody
# meant to close, and the next plan worked around it rather than reporting a
# rule it could not follow. The lesson is written into `CLAUDE.md`: a rule that
# lives in a document is one somebody has to notice being broken.
#
# `commit-msg` runs after the message is written and is handed its path, so the
# gate can read what the commit says about itself. Everything else is the same:
# it still runs before the commit is made, and a non-zero exit still stops it.
#
# What runs depends on where you are, what you changed, and now on what the
# message says. `scripts/which-checks.sh` decides it and is tested.
#
# `--no-verify` skips this hook as it skipped the last one, and the case it used
# to exist for is now handled: a RED commit says so in its message and is held
# to exactly the failures it names.
#
# Whoever merges a branch into main runs `scripts/check.sh all` first.
set -euo pipefail

root="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"
exec "$root/scripts/check.sh" --message-file="$1"
